"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_en"][17]={"university_electrical_engineering_en:M17-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M17-L1 – Baseband, Carriers and Modulation Models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Bachelor: Communication Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L1<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L6<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Map a bit sequence to complex\nsymbols, distinguish carrier signals from bandwidth, and normalise\nconstellations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From Information to Waveform<\/h2>\n<p>A digital message initially consists of bits. A transmitter groups\nthem, maps them to symbols and generates a continuous-time waveform. The\nchannel modifies this waveform; the receiver must reconstruct the\ntransmitted bits from an observed waveform. Source coding\n(representation of information) and channel coding (redundancy for error\ncorrection) are separate stages. We initially consider uncoded symbols\nwithout packet overhead.<\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nequally frequent symbols and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mi>k<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=2^k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none symbol carries\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbits. For symbol duration\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_s=1/T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_b=kR_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA symbol is not an individual voltage pulse: it can represent a pair of\northogonal signal components at a carrier frequency. Bit rate and symbol\nrate are identical only for binary modulation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Complex Baseband<\/h2>\n<p>With a real pulse\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand complex symbols\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_n=I_n+jQ_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe write<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Re<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(t)=\\sum_n a_n p(t-nT),\\qquad x(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{Re}\\{u(t)e^{j2\\pi f_ct}\\}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The real radio-frequency waveform consists of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{2}(I(t)\\cos 2\\pi f_ct-Q(t)\\sin 2\\pi f_ct)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe sign of the Q branch is a convention that the receiver must\npreserve. With sufficiently separated positive and negative spectral\ncomponents, the chosen scaling preserves energy between baseband and\npassband. The complex representation is a mathematical model, not an\nimaginary voltage at the antenna output.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/000178_en.svg\"\nalt=\"BPSK, QPSK and 16-QAM in the I/Q plane with equal mean symbol energy.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">BPSK, QPSK and 16-QAM in the I/Q plane\nwith equal mean symbol energy.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Constellations and Energy<\/h2>\n<p>BPSK uses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=\\pm1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(bit 0 → −1, bit 1 → +1). QPSK uses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>±<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=(\\pm1\\pm j)/\\sqrt{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\none bit each determines the sign of the I and Q components. 16-QAM uses\nfour amplitudes per axis: −3, −1, +1, +3. For equally probable points,\nthe mean energy of the unnormalised constellation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2(9+1+1+9)/4=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDividing by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mn>10<\/mn><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{10}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives a mean symbol energy of 1. For a unit-energy pulse these are\nnormalised energies; actual energies follow from physical scaling.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scheme<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Bits/symbol<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Minimum distance at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_s=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>BPSK<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>QPSK<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16-QAM<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>10<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2/\\sqrt{10}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>With Gray mapping, only one bit changes between immediately adjacent\npoints. For each 16-QAM axis, we use −3 → 00, −1 → 01, +1 → 11, +3 → 10.\nThis does not alter the geometric decision regions, but it changes the\nnumber of erroneous bits in an incorrect decision. 16-QAM also has\nvariable symbol amplitude; its peak energy is 1.8 for a mean of 1\n(before pulse overlap). A nonlinear amplifier can therefore introduce\nerrors different from those of a purely additive noise model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Rate and Bandwidth as Design Variables<\/h2>\n<p>At 100 kSym/s, the raw bit rates are 100, 200 or 400 kbit/s. With\nraised-cosine pulse shaping and roll-off\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe total positive RF bandwidth is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>125<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=(1+\\alpha)R_s=125<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkHz in the ideal model (see <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L3<\/a>). At\nthe same rate of 200 kbit/s, BPSK requires 250 kHz, QPSK 125 kHz and\n16-QAM 62.5 kHz. However, the decision also depends on transmitted\nenergy per bit and error rate, not just on the spectrum. More bits per\nsymbol mean less energy per bit at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe model in the next lesson explicitly distinguishes these comparison\nconditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Calculate the gross bit rate for 16-QAM at 250 kSym/s.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> 1 Mbit/s.<\/li>\n<li>Encode 1011 using the specified 16-QAM mapping (I first).\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>10<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3+j)/\\sqrt{10}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/li>\n<li>Why is QPSK divided by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> Without normalisation, every point would have\nenergy 2 rather than 1.<\/li>\n<li>A 200-kbit/s transmitter uses QPSK and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.35<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.35<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_s=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkSym/s,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>135<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=135<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkHz ideally.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-450-principles-of-digital-communications-i-fall-2006/pages/syllabus/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – Principles of Digital Communications I, topics 11–19\nand 20–23<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-450-principles-of-digital-communication-i-fall-2009/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – Principles of Digital Communication I, Lecture\nNotes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_en:M17-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M17-L2 – Channel, Noise and Detection<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Bachelor: Communication Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L1<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L6<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Normalise an AWGN model, derive\ndecision boundaries and calculate bit error rates.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The Model at the Decision Stage<\/h2>\n<p>After ideal pulse shaping, receive filtering and symbol sampling, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_n=a_n+w_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe assume correct timing and carrier synchronisation. Additive white\nGaussian noise (AWGN) has a two-sided power spectral density of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_0/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the continuous real-valued model. The noise components projected onto\northonormal real signal axes are independent and normally distributed\nwith variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_0/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nComplex baseband therefore requires precisely this variance per axis,\nnot\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\njointly,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E|w|^2=N_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor BPSK, the real axis suffices.<\/p>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_s=E|a|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_b=E_s/k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor uncoded data. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma_b=E_b/N_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand normalised\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_s=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>k<\/mi><msub><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2=\\frac{N_0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2k\\gamma_b},\\quad \\gamma_b=10^{(E_b/N_0)_{\\mathrm{dB}}/10}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At 4 dB with QPSK,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.512<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma_b=2.512<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1991<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_0=0.1991<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.3155<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma\\approx0.3155<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA 4 dB energy ratio is not converted by dividing by 20. In real\nreceivers, noise at the decision stage depends on the receive filter;\narbitrary noise per ADC sample does not automatically correspond to this\nmodel.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Minimum-Distance Decisions<\/h2>\n<p>For equally probable symbols and isotropic Gaussian noise, the\nmaximum-likelihood decision is also the maximum-a-posteriori decision.\nAs the density is proportional to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exp(-|r-a|^2/N_0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe receiver chooses<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒜<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat a=\\arg\\min_{a\\in\\mathcal A}|r-a|^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For BPSK the boundary is at zero, for QPSK along both axes, and for\nnormalised 16-QAM along the lines\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>10<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>10<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2/\\sqrt{10},0,2/\\sqrt{10}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non each axis. With unequal priors or correlated noise, Euclidean\ndistance and fixed midpoints are not generally optimal. A hard decision\ndiscards information about the distance to the boundaries; soft\ninformation can assist a channel decoder.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/000179_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Received 16-QAM points at 8 dB and theoretical bit error rates for BPSK, QPSK and 16-QAM.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Received 16-QAM points at 8 dB and\ntheoretical bit error rates for BPSK, QPSK and 16-QAM.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Error Rate and Theoretical Reference<\/h2>\n<p>For the Gaussian tail probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msubsup><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(x)=\\frac1{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}\\int_x^\\infty e^{-t^2/2}\\,dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe following holds for coherent BPSK: if +1 is transmitted, an error\noccurs precisely when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w&lt;-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBy symmetry,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_b=Q(\\sqrt{2\\gamma_b})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nQPSK separates into two independent BPSK decisions with the same bit\nenergy and has the same bit error rate. The QPSK symbol error rate is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-(1-P_b)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>For Gray-coded 16-QAM, we can average over the four possible\namplitudes and all decision regions on each axis. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>0.8<\/mn><msub><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=\\sqrt{0.8\\gamma_b}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>3<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_b=\\tfrac34 Q(d)+\\tfrac12 Q(3d)-\\tfrac14 Q(5d).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The common high-SNR approximation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>3<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac34 Q(d)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nneglects incorrect decisions farther away. The exact equation follows by\ncounting Hamming distances: the outer point −3 has expected erroneous\nbit count\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(d)+Q(3d)-Q(5d)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe inner point −1 has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2Q(d)+Q(3d)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSymmetry gives the two positive points. Averaging and dividing by two\nbits yields the formula.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Measuring Rather Than Just Observing an Error<\/h2>\n<p>The empirical BER is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_{\\mathrm{fehler}}/N_{\\mathrm{bits}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(subscript fehler denotes errors). With 10 errors in 10000 bits it is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{-3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbut this is only an estimate. Under an independent bit error model, the\nstandard deviation of the estimator is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{p(1-p)/N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA 16-QAM symbol decision can affect several bits simultaneously; the\nindependent bit model is therefore only an approximation in this case.\nZero observed errors are not proof of error-free transmission; with\nindependent errors, the approximate one-sided 95% upper bound is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3/N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the presence of error bursts, several independent segments must be\nexamined. BER, packet error rate and EVM (normalised squared deviation\nfrom reference symbols) are distinct measures.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>For BPSK at 0 dB,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma_b=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.07865<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_b=Q(\\sqrt2)\\approx0.07865<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/li>\n<li>What is the variance per axis for normalised 16-QAM at 0 dB?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/li>\n<li>For QPSK with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_b=0.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> symbol error rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mn>0.99<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0199<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-0.99^2=0.0199<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/li>\n<li>No errors in 100000 independent bits: <strong>Solution:<\/strong>\napproximate upper 95% bound\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\cdot10^{-5}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthis does not establish that BER is zero.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-450-principles-of-digital-communications-i-fall-2006/pages/syllabus/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – Principles of Digital Communications I, topics 11–19\nand 20–23<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-450-principles-of-digital-communication-i-fall-2009/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – Principles of Digital Communication I, Lecture\nNotes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_en:M17-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M17-L3 – Pulse Shaping and Intersymbol Interference<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Bachelor: Communication Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L1<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Apply the Nyquist criterion to\nan overall pulse, and weigh bandwidth, filter length and timing\ntolerance against one another.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why Rectangular Pulses Are Not Enough<\/h2>\n<p>A symbol stream can be modelled as a weighted impulse train. The\ntransmit filter generates continuous-time pulses from it. The channel\nand receive filter change their shape. The overall pulse\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncombines all three transfer functions; for linear time-invariant stages,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>*<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>*<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=p_T*h_C*p_R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRectangular pulses in simple NRZ signals have finite duration, but a\ntheoretically infinitely extended spectrum. A sharply limited frequency\nresponse in turn produces long tails in time.<\/p>\n<p>After delay compensation, sampling at the symbol instant gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r(mT)=a_m g(0)+\\sum_{n\\ne m}a_n g((m-n)T)+w_m.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The second term is intersymbol interference (ISI). With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(0)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(kT)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor every integer\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit vanishes. Pulses may overlap in time; their sum at the correct\nsampling instants is what matters. This is the first Nyquist criterion\nin the time domain. In the ideal low-pass case, the minimum one-sided\nbaseband bandwidth is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(2T)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nit must not be confused with the Nyquist sampling frequency of an\nADC.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Raised Cosine<\/h2>\n<p>An ideal overall pulse satisfying the criterion is given, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=t/T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nby<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sinc<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>α<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>α<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sinc<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(t)=\\operatorname{sinc}(x)\\frac{\\cos(\\pi\\alpha x)}{1-(2\\alpha x)^2},\\qquad \\operatorname{sinc}(x)=\\frac{\\sin(\\pi x)}{\\pi x}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\pm1/(2\\alpha)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe continuous limiting values must be used: 1 and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sinc<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\pi/4)\\operatorname{sinc}(1/(2\\alpha))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nrespectively. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe sinc pulse remains. The Fourier transform is constant in baseband up\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1-\\alpha)/(2T)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>),\nfalls over half a cosine period and is zero from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1+\\alpha)/(2T)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nonwards. For a real RF channel, the bandwidth around the positive\ncarrier frequency is therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1+\\alpha)/T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe negative spectral component is the mirror image, not a second usable\nchannel.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/000180_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Raised-cosine pulses, corresponding spectra and superimposed BPSK signal segments for two roll-off factors.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Raised-cosine pulses, corresponding\nspectra and superimposed BPSK signal segments for two roll-off\nfactors.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transmit and Receive Filters<\/h2>\n<p>In a flat channel, the transmitter can use a root-raised-cosine (RRC)\npulse with frequency response\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{G(f)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe time-reversed conjugate receive pulse is the matched filter; the\nproduct of both frequency responses is then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G(f)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe overall pulse has the Nyquist zeros; the transmit pulse alone need\nnot have them. In white noise, the matched filter maximises the SNR for\na known pulse at the intended instant. It does not automatically correct\na frequency-selective channel and assumes an appropriate pulse shape and\ntime reference.<\/p>\n<p>A finite FIR filter approximates the infinite pulse. At eight samples\nper symbol and a symmetric length of 81 coefficients (ten symbol\nintervals from −5T to +5T), the group delay of an individual filter is\n40 samples or 5T. For two such filters, it is 10T. Normalisation,\ntruncated tails and quantisation can cause residual ISI. Oversampling is\na numerical design variable, not a new bit rate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The Eye as a Diagnostic Tool<\/h2>\n<p>An eye diagram superimposes many time segments, typically two symbol\nperiods long. For binary real symbols, the values at ideal sampling\ninstants are −1 and +1. The vertical separation between the groups is\nthe noise margin in the interference-free model. The horizontal opening\nindicates how far the sampling instant can deviate from the optimum\nwhile the groups remain distinguishable. A more closed eye can result\nfrom bandwidth limitation, noise, reflections or timing errors; it does\nnot establish a specific cause.<\/p>\n<p>The illustrated eyes use a finite raised-cosine overall pulse and a\nrandom BPSK sequence without noise; edge effects are excluded. Both\nroll-off factors approximately satisfy the same sampling condition. The\nlarger factor occupies more spectrum but has shorter tails. Sensitivity\nalso depends on the channel and data pattern; the eye gives only a\nvisual estimate, not an exact BER without a statistical model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_s=200<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkSym/s,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> one-sided bandwidth 120 kHz, RF bandwidth 240\nkHz.<\/li>\n<li>Why does a Nyquist pulse not have to be confined to the interval\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> The zeros at the other sampling instants are\nsufficient with ideal timing.<\/li>\n<li>Two FIR filters with 49 coefficients each and six samples/symbol.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> combined delay 48 samples = 8T.<\/li>\n<li>What happens when sampling is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.2<\/mn><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.2T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntoo late? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> The other pulses are generally no\nlonger sampled at their zeros; data dependence and ISI result.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-450-principles-of-digital-communications-i-fall-2006/pages/syllabus/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – Principles of Digital Communications I, topics 11–19\nand 20–23<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://users.ece.utexas.edu/~bevans/courses/realtime/lectures/laboratory/c6748winDSK/lab5/index.html\">University\nof Texas at Austin – EE 445S, Lab 5: Digital Data Transmission by\nBaseband PAM<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_en:M17-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M17-L4 – Synchronisation, Multipath Channels and Equalisation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Bachelor: Communication Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L2<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish timing, frequency\nand phase errors, and quantitatively assess linear equalisation of a\nsimple channel.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Synchronisation as a Prerequisite<\/h2>\n<p>A receiver knows neither the start of the data block nor necessarily\nits timing, frequency and phase. A symbol timing error\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nshifts the samples to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(kT+\\tau)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\neven small deviations produce ISI when pulses overlap. A frequency error\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nby contrast, produces a time-dependent rotation with ideal timing:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_n=a_n e^{j(2\\pi\\Delta f nT+\\phi)}+w_n.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At 100 kSym/s and a frequency error of 500 Hz, the constellation\nrotates by 1.8° per symbol; after 50 symbol intervals it has rotated by\n90°. A one-off phase correction does not eliminate a frequency error.\nPreambles can provide coarse estimates of packet start, timing and\nfrequency; pilots and tracking loops follow the remaining deviations.\nWith unknown carrier phase, QPSK has an ambiguity of multiples of 90°,\nwhich known symbols or suitable coding must resolve. Timing recovery,\nfrequency estimation and framing are logically distinct tasks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A Discrete Multipath Model<\/h2>\n<p>Reflections from buildings or transmission-line ends produce delayed\nsignal components. The continuous channel can be described by a sum of\ndelayed paths. After filtering and sampling, we use the effective\nsymbol-rate channel<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>ℓ<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ℓ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_n=\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{L-1}h_\\ell a_{n-\\ell}+w_n.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The taps are generally complex and time-varying. A path with a\nfractional-symbol delay is usually distributed across several taps after\nfiltering. For a stationary example, we choose real coefficients\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_0=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_1=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(z)=1+0.6z^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTwo successive identical BPSK symbols produce a magnitude of 1.6;\nopposite symbols produce a magnitude of 0.4. Both remain distinguishable\nwithout noise, but the noise margin depends on the data.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|H(e^{j\\omega})|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlies between 0.4 and 1.6. A uniform gain cannot compensate for this\nfrequency response.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/000181_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Two-tap channel, magnitude response and BPSK samples before and after recursive equalisation.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two-tap channel, magnitude response and\nBPSK samples before and after recursive equalisation.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Zero Forcing and Noise Enhancement<\/h2>\n<p>If the channel is known, an ideal zero-forcing equaliser can invert\nits effect. Here,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G(z)=\\frac1{1+0.6z^{-1}},\\qquad y_n=r_n-0.6y_{n-1}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With both systems initially in the zero state, the output without\nnoise is exactly\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe feedback uses previous <strong>continuous-valued equaliser\noutputs<\/strong>, not hard symbol decisions. The impulse response\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.6<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-0.6)^m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis absolutely summable, so this causal inverse filter is stable. For\nindependent white noise with variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe steady-state result is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mo accent=\"false\">∞<\/mo><\/munderover><msup><mn>0.6<\/mn><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.36<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5625<\/mn><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(y_n-a_n)=\\sigma^2\\sum_{m=0}^\\infty0.6^{2m}=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{1-0.36}=1.5625\\sigma^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The mean noise variance increases by 1.94 dB. A channel with a zero\non the unit circle does not permit bounded exact inversion of this kind;\na non-minimum-phase channel does not permit stable causal exact\ninversion without further measures. An MMSE equaliser accepts residual\ndistortion in order to limit the overall mean squared error. For known\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhite symbols of variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand white noise with a flat spectrum, the unconstrained\nfrequency-by-frequency solution is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>H<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=H^*/(|H|^2+N_0/E_s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the complex model. A realisable finite filter length and desired\ndelay must be considered separately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Channel Estimation and Testing<\/h2>\n<p>With a known training sequence, a tapped-delay model can be written\nas a linear system of equations\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒉<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒘<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf r=\\mathbf A\\mathbf h+\\mathbf w<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe rows of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontain current and delayed training symbols. With full column rank, the\nleast-squares estimate minimises the squared norm of the residual;\nnumerically, QR or SVD should be used rather than explicit inversion of\nthe normal equations. A constant training sequence cannot reliably\ndistinguish the taps. An independent test sequence checks whether the\nestimated channel also applies outside the training data. In the model\nexperiment <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L5<\/a>, we\ndeliberately assume that the channel is known in order to isolate the\neffect of inversion.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta f=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nHz at 10 kSym/s. <strong>Solution:<\/strong> 3.6° rotation per symbol\ninterval.<\/li>\n<li>Calculate the two-tap output for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_{n-1}=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_n=+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> 0.4 before noise is added.<\/li>\n<li>Replace 0.6 with 0.9. <strong>Solution:<\/strong> Noise variance\nfactor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.81<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.263<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(1-0.81)=5.263<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor 7.21 dB; inversion becomes more sensitive.<\/li>\n<li>Why can a known pilot symbol resolve a QPSK phase ambiguity?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> It provides a reference for the absolute\norientation of the constellation.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-450-principles-of-digital-communication-i-fall-2009/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – Principles of Digital Communication I, Lecture\nNotes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://users.ece.utexas.edu/~bevans/courses/realtime/lectures/laboratory/c6748winDSK/lab5/index.html\">University\nof Texas at Austin – EE 445S, Lab 5: Digital Data Transmission by\nBaseband PAM<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_en:M17-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M17-L5 – Comparing Transmission Systems<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Bachelor: Communication Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L1<\/a> to <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compare modulation schemes at\nequal bit energy and equal gross bit rate, and document a justified\nsystem decision.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Requirements for a Modelled Data Link<\/h2>\n<p>A civilian telemetry link is to transmit uncoded data at a gross rate\nof 200 kbit/s. The ideal model specifies a maximum occupied RF bandwidth\nof 150 kHz, a BER below\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{-3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand 8 dB at the ideally synchronised AWGN detector. This is neither a\nreal radio standard nor a complete link budget; transmit power,\npropagation, packet headers, channel coding and noise figure have not\nyet been specified. The question is: which of the three schemes meets\nall three model requirements simultaneously?<\/p>\n<p>For all schemes, we assume overall raised-cosine pulse shaping with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe symbol energy is normalised to 1 for each scheme; the noise variance\nis adjusted according to <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L2<\/a> to\nachieve equal bit energy relative to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe experiment simulates the sufficient symbol samples, not the DAC, RF\ncarrier or practical RRC filters. The bandwidth is calculated from the\ntheoretical pulse model. At the same gross bit rate and the same\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_b/N_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe physical mean received power can be the same for equal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbecause\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=E_bR_b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe symbol energy is then proportional to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Experiment and Reproducible Code<\/h2>\n<p>The same 400,000 random bits are used for each scheme. New normally\ndistributed values are generated for each of the three noise levels.\nBPSK and QPSK have the same theoretical BER at equal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_b/N_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\n16-QAM uses Gray mapping and minimum-distance detection. The script\nfirst checks error-free demapping and mean energy, then the deviation\nfrom the analytical BER. The final part simulates BPSK in the known\ntwo-tap channel from M17-L4 at 6 dB. For this, 100,000 bits and the same\nnoise values for all three comparison cases are used. NumPy is the only\nexternal dependency.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> math <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> erfc, sqrt<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rng <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"dv\">1705<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>N <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">400_000<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>bits <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> rng.integers(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, N)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>gray <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>, <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>Q <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> x: <span class=\"fl\">0.5<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> erfc(x <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rows <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> []<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> k <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    b <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> bits.reshape(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, k)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> k <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        s <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>b[:, <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">elif<\/span> k <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        s <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>b[:, <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span> <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"ot\">1j<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>b[:, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>))<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        s <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (gray[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>b[:, <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>b[:, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]] <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"ot\">1j<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>gray[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>b[:, <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>b[:, <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>]])<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> detect(r):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> k <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> (r.real <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>).astype(<span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>)[:, <span class=\"va\">None<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> k <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> np.column_stack((r.real <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, r.imag <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)).astype(<span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        i <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.argmin(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(r.real[:, <span class=\"va\">None<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>gray), axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        q <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.argmin(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(r.imag[:, <span class=\"va\">None<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>gray), axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> np.column_stack((i<span class=\"op\">//<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, i<span class=\"op\">%<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, q<span class=\"op\">//<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, q<span class=\"op\">%<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.array_equal(detect(s), b)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(np.mean(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(s)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.015<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> db <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-28\"><a href=\"#cb1-28\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        g <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">10<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span>(db<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-29\"><a href=\"#cb1-29\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        sigma <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>k<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>g))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-30\"><a href=\"#cb1-30\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        n <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> sigma<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>rng.standard_normal(s.size)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-31\"><a href=\"#cb1-31\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> k <span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-32\"><a href=\"#cb1-32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            n <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> n <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"ot\">1j<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>sigma<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>rng.standard_normal(s.size)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-33\"><a href=\"#cb1-33\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        errors <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.count_nonzero(detect(s<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>n) <span class=\"op\">!=<\/span> b)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-34\"><a href=\"#cb1-34\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        a <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> sqrt(<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>g)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-35\"><a href=\"#cb1-35\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        theory <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> Q(sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>g)) <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> k <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.75<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>Q(a)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>Q(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>a)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.25<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>Q(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>a)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-36\"><a href=\"#cb1-36\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"co\"># Binomial approximation as a broad numerical sanity check, not a CI.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-37\"><a href=\"#cb1-37\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(errors<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>N<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>theory) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">7<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>sqrt(theory<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>theory)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>N)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">5<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>N<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-38\"><a href=\"#cb1-38\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        rows.append((k, db, errors, errors<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>N, theory))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-39\"><a href=\"#cb1-39\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"co\"># Two-tap known channel; zero-state recursive linear inverse, no decisions fed back.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-40\"><a href=\"#cb1-40\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>b <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> bits[:<span class=\"dv\">100_000<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> s <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>b<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-41\"><a href=\"#cb1-41\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>h <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> clean <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.convolve(s, [<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, h])[:<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(s)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-42\"><a href=\"#cb1-42\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>n <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span>(<span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span>)))<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>rng.standard_normal(<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(s))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-43\"><a href=\"#cb1-43\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> inverse(r):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-44\"><a href=\"#cb1-44\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    z <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.empty(<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(r))<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> prev <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-45\"><a href=\"#cb1-45\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> j, v <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">enumerate<\/span>(r):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-46\"><a href=\"#cb1-46\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        prev <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> v<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>h<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>prev<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> z[j] <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> prev<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-47\"><a href=\"#cb1-47\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> z<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-48\"><a href=\"#cb1-48\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(inverse(clean)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>s)) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1e-12<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-49\"><a href=\"#cb1-49\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>r <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> clean<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>n<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> z <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> inverse(r)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-50\"><a href=\"#cb1-50\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>isi <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [np.mean((v<span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">!=<\/span>b) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> v <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (s<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>n, r, z)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-51\"><a href=\"#cb1-51\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>ratio <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.var(z<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>s)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>np.var(n)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-52\"><a href=\"#cb1-52\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(ratio<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>h<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>h)) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.04<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-53\"><a href=\"#cb1-53\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"va\">__name__<\/span> <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;__main__&#39;<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-54\"><a href=\"#cb1-54\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> row <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> rows: <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>row)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-55\"><a href=\"#cb1-55\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;ISI:&#39;<\/span>, isi, <span class=\"st\">&#39;noise ratio:&#39;<\/span>, ratio)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Results and Selection<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_b/N_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">BPSK BER (errors)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">QPSK BER (errors)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">16-QAM BER (errors)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0 dB<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.078885 (31554)<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.0777125 (31085)<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.1396625 (55865)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4 dB<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.0125875 (5035)<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.012580 (5032)<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.0586825 (23473)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8 dB<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.0001575 (63)<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.000200 (80)<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.009190 (3676)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>At 8 dB, the theoretical BPSK/QPSK value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.909<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.909\\cdot10^{-4}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the exact Gray-coded 16-QAM value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9.247<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9.247\\cdot10^{-3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Monte Carlo values fluctuate around their references. The broad test\nlimit used in the code is a numerical plausibility check and not a\nformal confidence test. A reproducible random seed does not replace a\nmeasurement campaign.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-28\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-28\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scheme<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Symbol rate (kSym/s)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Ideal RF bandwidth (kHz)<\/th>\n<th>Requirement: 150 kHz / BER &lt; 0.001<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>BPSK<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">200<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">250<\/td>\n<td>Bandwidth requirement not met<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>QPSK<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">100<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">125<\/td>\n<td>Both requirements met<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16-QAM<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">50<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">62.5<\/td>\n<td>BER requirement not met<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/000182_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Simulated bit error rates, theoretical comparison curves, bandwidth at equal gross bit rate and errors in the multipath channel.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Simulated bit error rates, theoretical\ncomparison curves, bandwidth at equal gross bit rate and errors in the\nmultipath channel.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>QPSK is the suitable starting point for these requirements. A\ndifference of 25 kHz from the model bandwidth limit is not a guaranteed\nguard band for a real transmitter. 16-QAM would be of interest at a\nhigher energy ratio: for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_b=10^{-3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe high-SNR approximation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.001333<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(d)\\approx0.001333<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.004<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\approx3.004<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>11.28<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma_b\\approx11.28<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(10.52 dB). QPSK theoretically requires about 6.79 dB for the same\ntarget value; at 8 dB, approximately 1.21 dB of margin remains in this\nAWGN model. At 200 kbit/s, a fair energy comparison must therefore not\nbe confused with identical noise standard deviations in the normalised\nsymbol model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Impairment Scenario and Limitations<\/h2>\n<p>In the two-tap experiment at 6 dB, BPSK gives a BER of 0.00230\nwithout an echo, 0.06545 with an echo and no equaliser, and 0.01168 with\nrecursive equalisation. The empirical noise variance enhancement factor\nis 1.562506, virtually identical to the theoretical factor of 1.5625.\nInversion removes ISI in the absence of noise but enhances noise; it\ndoes not restore the original ideal BER. This additional measurement is\nnot a QPSK channel test and does not permit a direct conclusion about\nthe QPSK specification. It demonstrates why the AWGN-only selection must\nbe supplemented by channel and synchronisation tests.<\/p>\n<p>To extend the work to a real link, an RRC transmit/receive model of\nfinite length, training and channel estimation, sampling and frequency\nerrors, power amplifiers and a spectrum measurement are needed. Packet\nerror rate also depends on block length and error correlation. Even with\nindependent errors, 1000 bits at a BER of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\cdot10^{-4}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhave a probability of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.0002<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>18.1<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-(1-0.0002)^{1000}\\approx18.1\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof containing at least one error. Channel coding, CRC and retransmission\nare therefore separate system decisions. The radio-link parameters are\nexplored further in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;page=study-path&amp;section=S3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M18 –\nAntennas<\/a> once that module has been developed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>With a bandwidth of 125 kHz and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_s=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkSym/s; 16-QAM theoretically carries a gross bit rate of 400\nkbit/s.<\/li>\n<li>Determine the 16-QAM BER at 8 dB from the table.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3676<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>400000<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.00919<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3676/400000=0.00919<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nmore than nine times the target value.<\/li>\n<li>A code rate of 3/4 is introduced. <strong>Solution:<\/strong> To\nmaintain 200 kbit/s of payload bits (without further overhead), a gross\nrate of 266.67 kbit/s is needed. QPSK then requires 166.67 kHz; the\noriginal bandwidth limit would be exceeded. The BER of the coded system\nmust be recalculated.<\/li>\n<li>Why is a zero-forcing equaliser problematic at a channel null?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> It would require unbounded gain at that\npoint; even small channel magnitudes lead to substantial noise\nenhancement.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-450-principles-of-digital-communications-i-fall-2006/pages/syllabus/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – Principles of Digital Communications I, Topics 11–19\nand 20–23<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-450-principles-of-digital-communication-i-fall-2009/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – Principles of Digital Communication I, Lecture\nNotes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://users.ece.utexas.edu/~bevans/courses/realtime/lectures/laboratory/c6748winDSK/lab5/index.html\">University\nof Texas at Austin – EE 445S, Lab 5: Digital Data Transmission by\nBaseband PAM<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
